WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.410
- [Narrator] These trucks stretching

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.480
to the horizon are only a fraction

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.190
of the 5.5 million that
entered Laredo, Texas in 2022.

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.020
It's one sign of a broader shift

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.000
in the global economy as hundreds

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.580
of companies around the world
move their manufacturing

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.220
from China to here in Mexico,

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.830
and as a border town,
that makes Laredo the hub

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.430
at the center of a trend
that's changing global markets.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.650
- If you are manufacturing in Mexico,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.580
you can get that product

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.700
to the US consumer in
under 24 hours in a truck.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.220
If you are manufacturing in Asia,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.190
it's gonna take you two to six weeks

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.720
- [Narrator] Amid an explosion of trade

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.120
between Mexico and the
rest of North America,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.730
here's a look at why Laredo
is primed to become one

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.520
of the world's most important
land ports and its role

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.593
in transforming global supply chains.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.750
On this bridge dividing
the US from Mexico,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.310
trucks often wait hours

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.560
for a chance to cross into Laredo.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.460
In 2022, more than $800
million worth of products,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.310
from auto parts to carpets to avocados

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.110
passed through the city each day

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.880
according to US Census data.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.970
- The total trade between
Mexico and the United States,

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.210
40% of that crosses
through the port of Laredo.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.380
- [Narrator] Daniel Covarrubias

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.270
has spent years studying Laredo's role

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.410
in international trade

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.930
at Texas A&amp;M International University.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.030
- If you see on a map
where Laredo is situated

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.970
between the United States, the
biggest market in the world

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.400
and Mexico one of the
biggest producing markets

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.070
in the world, well, it's
right in the middle of it.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.900
- [Narrator] Laredo is uniquely positioned

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.250
to handle all this trade

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.500
as more and more companies
reduce their dependence

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.830
on Chinese factories and
shift production to Mexico.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.653
It's part of a larger
strategy called nearshoring.

00:01:41.506 --> 00:01:45.063
- It went from a trend of
globalization to regionalization.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.870
- Economists and executives
say soaring shipping rates,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.000
a trade war between China and the US

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.880
and geopolitical uncertainty
caused by Russia's invasion

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.450
of Ukraine helped fuel
the nearshoring trend.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.270
Plus, Mexico's free trade
deals mean companies

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.460
that make their products
in Mexico can ship them

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.040
to the rest of North America duty free.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.370
- You're seeing Chinese companies
to get out of the tariffs

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.460
and post on them, well, they
can come and take advantage

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.920
of the US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.680
if they manufacture in Mexico.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.720
- [Instructor] In 2022, goods traded

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.070
between the US and Mexico
reached almost $780 billion,

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.370
an increase of nearly 18%
from the previous year.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.490
While Laredo isn't the only
US border town capitalizing

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.290
on the expansion of nearshoring,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.033
it's receiving the most activity.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.530
In October 2022, about $27 billion worth

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.990
of freight moved through Laredo alone.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.940
That month, it overtook the trade volume

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.230
from each of the ocean ports

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.720
of Los Angeles and Long Beach.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.470
Executives say part of Laredo's
appeal is its location.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.480
The city is the closest US land port

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.480
to some of Mexico's largest
manufacturing centers,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.070
like the Nuevo Leon region,

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.490
which includes the massive
industrial city of Monterrey.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.260
- From loading up on a truck in Monterrey,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.780
you have a two and a half hour drive north

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.930
from Monterrey until you
hit the Laredo border.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.610
- [Narrator] Tom Simmons
is the vice president

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.840
of Majestic Realty,

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.690
a privately owned industrial
real estate developer.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.980
Over the last 10 years,

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.850
Majestic has invested in warehouse
properties across Laredo.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.010
- That product will get unpacked

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.710
in a Laredo warehouse like
we're sitting in right now.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.400
Will get repacked into
outbound trucks and tractors

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.070
and will get distributed
throughout the United States,

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:29.903
and you'll have all

00:03:29.903 --> 00:03:32.825
of the roadway networks that
will spread east and west.

00:03:32.825 --> 00:03:34.680
- [Narrator] The expansion
of trade in Laredo

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:35.820
is fueled by the surge

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.530
of foreign direct investment in Mexico

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.060
over the last two decades.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.010
Mattel expanded its Monterrey plant

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.130
into its largest manufacturing
facility worldwide.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:47.730
LEGO also operates its
largest factory in Nuevo Leon

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:51.390
with plans to expand, and
Tesla recently announced plans

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.690
to build out a massive gigafactory
in the Monterrey region.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.760
And those are just a
small sample of companies

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.560
relocating their operations.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.320
The Mexican government said
more than 400 companies

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.160
have shown interest

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.680
in moving production from Asia to Mexico.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.210
That translates into a surge

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.270
of products funneled into
logistics hubs like Laredo.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.430
- If you take a 35-mile radius

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.650
and place it here on this
cross-border region of Laredo,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.560
and Nuevo Laredo, you're
gonna find upwards

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:20.850
of 500 US custom brokers and
Mexican custom brokers firms.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.790
You're gonna find more than
500 transportation companies,

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.280
more than 350 logistics companies.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.320
- [Narrator] That concentration
of logistics companies

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.630
means trucks,
(truck rumbling)

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:31.650
and lots of them.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.250
- More than 15,000 trucks cross daily

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.390
by the port of Laredo

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.700
and this is between two
bridges, the World Trade Bridge

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.170
and the Colombia Bridge.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.460
- [Narrator] The amount of trucks passing

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.500
through Laredo has seen a steady increase

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.240
over the last 10 years.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.140
To keep up,

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.660
large infrastructure
build-outs are underway.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:49.950
The city has already committed

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:53.580
to expanding the World Trade
Bridge from eight lanes to 16

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.580
and the rail company,
Canadian Pacific Kansas City,

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.400
broke ground on a project
that will double the capacity

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.130
of a rail bridge spanning the Rio Grande.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.430
More trade passing through
Laredo also means greater demand

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:06.780
for warehouse space.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:07.800
In this warehouse,

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.230
thousands of auto parts
are transported in and out

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.403
by truck each day.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.440
Just one lot over,

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.330
trucks flatten the ground to make room

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.100
for even more facilities.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.540
- We're adjusting to the supply

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.800
and demand imbalance right now

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.930
with the simple answer of just build.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.610
- [Narrator] This site is just one example

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.010
of the more than 3.8 million square feet

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.620
of warehouse space under
construction in Laredo.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.370
That amounts to a 28%
increase in space since 2012,

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.490
according to CoStar, a
real estate analytics firm.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.700
But with warehouses
more than 97% occupied,

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.580
companies are struggling
to keep up with demand.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.730
- It is actually an unhealthy
market because there're a lot

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.760
of businesses in this
market that want to grow.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.100
- [Narrator] As manufacturing
shifts south of the border,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.920
it is creating job
opportunities in the US.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.150
That's because components

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.790
for certain equipment
are often made in Mexico

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:59.970
but need a more skilled labor
force for final assembly.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.240
- The automotive and the
electronic will oftentimes go back

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.440
and forth across the Mexico-US
border four, five, six times

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.150
before the finished
product is finally ready

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:10.830
for the US consumer.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.810
- [Narrator] While the boom
in trade between the US

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:16.290
and Mexico might mean a smaller
slice of the pie for China,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.060
executives and economists say

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.160
it will still remain a dominant supplier

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.590
for a vast range of goods.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.080
In the meantime, the trade
passing through Laredo

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:26.310
is projected to grow.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.110
- What the numbers and data tell us

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.870
is that the port of Laredo
is gonna keep on growing.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:34.740
It's just so in a league in by itself

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.990
compared to other ports of entry,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.033
it's gonna be hard to compete with Laredo.

00:06:40.033 --> 00:06:42.700
(pensive music)

